# Auth Knowledge

Project-specific: built from the Notion pages "Регистрация", "Аутентификация", "Авторизация",
"RBAC" and `docs/decisions.md` (A1–A8, R1–R4). Where this file and a decision disagree, the
decision wins and this file is fixed.

## Model

- **Access token** — JWT HS256, 15 min (`AUTH_ACCESS_TTL_SECONDS=900`), cookie `access_token`.
  Claims: `sub` (user id), `typ: "access"`, `ver` (the user's `tokenVersion`), `iat`, `exp`.
- **Refresh token** — JWT HS256 signed with a **different** secret, 30 days
  (`AUTH_REFRESH_TTL_SECONDS=2592000`), cookie `refresh_token`. Claims: `sub`, `typ: "refresh"`,
  `ver`, `iat`, `exp`.
- **Nothing about refresh tokens is stored on the server**: no table, no `jti`, no session or
  family id, no allowlist or denylist (Notion "Авторизация"). Rotation means every successful
  refresh issues a **new pair**; the old refresh JWT simply expires.
- **Revocation without refresh storage** — `users.token_version` (int). Both guards reject a JWT
  whose `ver` differs from the user's current value or whose user is not `active`. It is
  incremented on account deletion, password reset and admin block. Logout does **not** touch it:
  logout = clearing the cookies (a stolen refresh JWT stays valid until `exp`, as the spec
  accepts).
- **Passwords** — argon2id (`argon2`), never logged, policy in `common/validation/password.schema.ts`
  (decision A8).

Lifetimes and secrets are config (`AUTH_ACCESS_*`, `AUTH_REFRESH_*`, `AUTH_JWT_ACCESS_SECRET`,
`AUTH_JWT_REFRESH_SECRET`), never constants.

## Cookies

| Cookie | Value | HttpOnly | Secure | SameSite | Path | Max-Age |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `access_token` | access JWT | yes | `config.auth.cookieSecure` | Lax | `/` | access TTL |
| `refresh_token` | refresh JWT | yes | `config.auth.cookieSecure` | Lax | `/api/auth/refresh` | refresh TTL |
| `csrf_token` | random 32 bytes | **no** | `config.auth.cookieSecure` | Lax | `/` | refresh TTL |

- Cookie Max-Age equals the token TTL (Notion: "TTL cookie синхронизирован с exp").
- The refresh cookie is path-scoped so it reaches only the refresh endpoint.
- Writing/clearing cookies lives in one `AuthCookies` helper; clearing uses the same attributes.
- Tokens are never in response bodies, URLs or logs.

## E-mail confirmation challenges

One `challenges` table serves every confirmable action (decision A4):

| Column | |
| --- | --- |
| `id` | uuid — returned to the client as `challengeId` |
| `purpose` | `registration` \| `login` \| `password_reset` \| `email_change` \| `account_deletion` |
| `user_id` | FK → users |
| `code_hash` | HMAC-SHA256(`AUTH_OTP_PEPPER`, 6-digit code) |
| `token_hash` | SHA-256 of the 32-byte magic-link token |
| `payload` | jsonb, e.g. `{ newEmail }` for `email_change` |
| `attempts` | wrong OTP entries |
| `expires_at` / `consumed_at` / `last_sent_at` / `created_at` | timestamptz |

- Code: `crypto.randomInt(0, 1_000_000)`, zero-padded. TTL 10 min, 5 wrong attempts → challenge
  locked (423 `AUTH_OTP_LOCKED`), resend not more often than once per 60 s (429 with
  `retryAfter`). A new challenge for the same user + purpose consumes the previous one.
- Magic link: `${APP_PUBLIC_URL}/api/auth/confirm?token=<token>` (the exact form Notion gives); single use; bound to its challenge.
- Every confirmation compares hashes with `crypto.timingSafeEqual` and sets `consumed_at` in the
  same transaction as the action it confirms.
- Which scenarios require confirmation is runtime config: `app_settings` keys
  `confirmation.registration`, `confirmation.login`, `confirmation.password_reset` (decision A6).
  `email_change` and `account_deletion` always require it (Notion).
- Codes and links travel only in mail (via the outbox) — never in logs, responses or test output
  other than the Mailpit sink.

## Endpoints (`/api/auth`)

| Endpoint | Auth | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `GET /csrf` | public | sets `csrf_token`, 204 |
| `POST /register` | public | confirmation off → 201 + user, cookies set; on → 202 `{ requiresConfirmation: true, challengeId }`, user `pending_verification` |
| `POST /login` | public | confirmation off → 200 + user, cookies set; on → 202 `{ requiresConfirmation: true, challengeId }`, no cookies |
| `POST /confirm` | public | `{ challengeId, code }` → completes the challenge's action (registration / login set cookies) |
| `GET /confirm?token=` | public | magic-link variant of `POST /confirm` |
| `POST /confirm/resend` | public | `{ challengeId }` → 202 |
| `POST /refresh` | public (refresh cookie) | validates the refresh JWT, issues a new pair, 204 |
| `POST /logout` | authenticated | clears cookies, 204 |
| `GET /api/me` | authenticated | 200 current user |
| `POST /password/forgot` | public | 202 always; mail with a `password_reset` challenge |
| `POST /password/reset` | public | `{ challengeId, code \| token, password }` → sets password, increments `token_version`, 204 |

## Refresh

`POST /refresh`: read `refresh_token`, verify signature with the refresh secret, `typ`, `exp`;
load the user; reject unless `status = active` and `ver = token_version`. Any failure → 401
`AUTH_REFRESH_INVALID` and both cookies cleared. Success → new access + refresh JWT, new cookies.
There are no further server-side checks (Notion: "Дополнительных серверных проверок по состоянию
refresh нет").

## CSRF — double submit

- `CsrfGuard` (global) runs on every POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE: `X-CSRF-Token` must equal the
  `csrf_token` cookie (constant-time compare), else 403 `CSRF_INVALID`.
- It applies to public endpoints too (login CSRF). The client calls `GET /api/auth/csrf` first;
  login, confirm and refresh re-issue it.
- `GET /api/auth/confirm?token=` is a GET and is therefore safe from CSRF by design; it must stay
  idempotent per token (second use → 400 `AUTH_CHALLENGE_INVALID`).

## Guards

Global order: `ThrottlerGuard → CsrfGuard → JwtAuthGuard → PermissionGuard` (`03-nestjs`).

- `JwtAuthGuard` reads `access_token` from the cookie, verifies it with the access secret, loads
  the user (one indexed query), checks `status = active` and `ver`, puts
  `AuthUser { id, roleIds }` on the request. Skipped by `@Public()`. Failure → 401
  `AUTH_UNAUTHENTICATED`.
- `PermissionGuard` checks `@RequirePermission(permission, action)` metadata against the RBAC
  cache for the user's roles; no metadata = any authenticated user. Failure → 403
  `AUTH_FORBIDDEN`.
- `@CurrentUser()` gives the handler the `AuthUser`; the controller passes it to the service.

## RBAC (DB-driven)

- Tables `roles` (`name` unique), `permissions` (`name` unique, `actions text[]`), `grants`
  (`role_id`, `permission_id`, `actions text[]` — empty = all actions of the permission; unique
  `(role_id, permission_id)`), `user_roles` (`user_id`, `role_id`).
- `RbacService` loads all roles, permissions and grants at startup into an immutable snapshot and
  replaces it after every successful admin write (`/api/admin/rbac/roles|permissions|grants`,
  CRUD per Notion). Check = any of the user's roles has a grant for the permission whose actions
  are empty or contain the action.
- Admin endpoints themselves require `@RequirePermission("rbac", "manage")`.
- Seed (decision R3): role `admin` with grants on every permission, role `user` with none. Every
  registered user gets `user`.

## Self vs permission

- A permission answers "may this user act on **other** users' resources". Self access to one's own
  profile, history and files needs no permission.
- The service decides, after loading the row:

```ts
const isSelf = actor.id === targetUserId;
if (!isSelf && !this.rbac.can(actor, "users", "read")) {
  throw new AppException(ErrorCode.AUTH_FORBIDDEN, HttpStatus.FORBIDDEN);
}
```

- For the Self-only resources Notion lists 403 for a foreign id (history download "запись не
  принадлежит пользователю" → 403); follow the page. Elsewhere prefer 404 when existence is a leak.

## Account enumeration

- Login failure is always 401 `AUTH_INVALID_CREDENTIALS`, same body and similar timing whether the
  email exists or not (verify against a dummy argon2 hash when the user is missing).
- Registration of a taken e-mail → 409 `USER_EMAIL_TAKEN` (decision A7). `password/forgot` and
  `confirm/resend` always answer 202.

## Rate limits (`@nestjs/throttler`)

| Endpoint | Limit (default) | Tracker |
| --- | --- | --- |
| login | 5 / min, 20 / hour | IP + normalised email |
| register, password/forgot, confirm/resend | 3 / min | IP |
| confirm, password/reset | 10 / min | IP |
| refresh | 30 / min | IP |
| everything else | 100 / min | user id or IP |

429 → `RATE_LIMITED` with `context.retryAfter`. `trust proxy` must be set behind a proxy.

## Don't

- Don't store refresh tokens, their hashes, `jti`s or session ids anywhere — database, cache, logs.
- Don't return tokens in response bodies; the browser never needs to read them.
- Don't compare secrets with `===`; use `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on equal-length buffers.
- Don't decide ownership in a guard.
- Don't hardcode role names in guards; routes declare permissions, roles get them through grants.
- Don't log emails in full, codes, tokens, cookies or password hashes.
